tus"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2180"} "National Olympic Nautical Stadium d'Île-de-France, Vaires-sur-Marne, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2008652"} "29 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "At Tokyo 2020, Hungary had gone 1-2, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/134360"} "Bálint Kopasz"] " taking gold ahead of his countryman " [:a {:href "/athletes/143671"} "Ádám Varga"] ". Varga was the favorite in 2024, ranking #1 in the world coming into Paris. He had taken silver in the event at the 2023 World Championships, behind Portugal’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/123193"} "Fernando Pimenta"] ". A sentimental favorite was Czechia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/125290"} "Josef Dostál"] ", who had won Olympic medals in 2012, 2016, and 2020, with three bronzes and a silver. The 2022 World Champion, a medal in Paris 2024 would make him only the seventh man to win medals in sprint canoe at four or more Olympic Games."] "\n\n" [:p {} "All the favorites advanced through the heats directly into the semi-finals. Kopasz and Varga showed their strength by winning the two semi-finals. Pimenta trailed Kopasz in the first semi, while Dostál was third in the second semi, behind Varga and the Argentine " [:a {:href "/athletes/140483"} "Agustín Vernice"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final was close between the three favorites, but Kopasz fell back near the end and would settle for bronze. Dostál pulled a mild upset, outlasting Varga for his first Olympic gold medal by 0.69 seconds. There was an unusual tie for fourth between Vernice and " [:a {:href "/athletes/151169"} "Uladzislau Kravets"] " (AIN), who both posted 3:28.10, more than four seconds behind Dostál."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Bib"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:c